nterp's marking register freed for the heap window base B (M6 step a (2), ADR-0033 decision 3). Step (3)'s decode
turns a 32-bit reference r into the address B | r, and every decode site needs B (the 4 GiB-aligned heap window
base) in a register. nterp is hand-written arm64 and decodes inline, so it needs B in a register it keeps live.
Our collectors run without read barriers — dalvikvm and the run-tests default to SemiSpace
(ART_DEFAULT_GC_TYPE_IS_SS) and apps run concurrent mark-sweep (app_process -Xgc:CMS, ADR-0033 decision 5) — so
nterp never takes the marking path and the marking register w20/x20 (main.S:63) is free. This patch does the two
behaviour-preserving halves of step (2):

  (a) TEST_IF_MARKING (main.S) becomes an empty macro: nterp never branches to a marking slow path.
  (b) REFRESH_MARKING_REGISTER (asm_support_arm64.S) loads the 64-bit base B into x20 from a new Thread field
      instead of the 32-bit GC-marking flag, so x20 == B wherever nterp runs guest code.

B reaches x20 through a new 64-bit Thread field heap_window_base, read with one xSELF-relative load, mirroring
what REFRESH_MARKING_REGISTER already did with THREAD_IS_GC_MARKING_OFFSET. The asm offset constant
THREAD_HEAP_WINDOW_BASE_OFFSET is generated by cpp-define-generator from Thread::HeapWindowBaseOffset(), so the
number is validated by the build and never hand-written. Thread::Init sets the field from gHeapWindowBase
(base/heap_window.h), which is fixed before the first thread attaches (the heap is created before the main thread
attaches, runtime.cc), so every thread's copy holds the same B.

read_barrier_config.h already defines RESERVE_MARKING_REGISTER unconditionally and sets kReserveMarkingRegister
true "for all GCs as nterp requires it", so x20 is reserved globally (step (3)'s compiled-code decode needs that
too) and IsNterpSupported() is already true for arm64 (kReserveMarkingRegister && !kUseTableLookupReadBarrier) —
no IsNterpSupported or build-config change is needed. In our no-read-barrier build the only reader of the marking
register is nterp's TEST_IF_MARKING, which (a) empties: the two cbnz wMR fast-exits in quick_entrypoints_arm64.S
sit inside USE_READ_BARRIER / USE_BAKER_READ_BARRIER and are not compiled without read barriers, and no C++ reads
the marking register either (SetIsGcMarkingAndUpdateEntrypoints belongs to the concurrent-copying collector alone,
the one GC type that uses read barriers, which is never enabled here; SemiSpace and concurrent mark-sweep both
leave is_gc_marking false). Thread::Init carries a static_assert so this repurposing fails the build loudly if the
GC is ever switched to a read-barrier collector.

Behaviour is preserved until step (3): x20's value is not consumed for correctness anywhere in this patch.

